Section name: Introduction
Excerpt:
The abundance of data, coupled with AI and advanced algorithms, has revolutionized almost every aspect of human life. As data-driven technologies take root in our lives, their drawbacks and potential harms become increasingly evident. Subsequently, algorithmic fair- ness has become central in computer science research to minimize machine bias. However, despite substantial focus on fairness in pre- dictive analysis and machine learning [ 6,36,39], traditional combi- natorial optimization has received limited attention [ 49]. Neverthe- less, combinatorial problems such as ranking [ 3], matching [ 19,20], resource allocation [ 9,17,27], and graph problems of influence maximization [ 46] and edge recommendation [ 8,43] have massive societal impact. In this paper, we study the Set Cover problem through the lens of fairness. As one of Karp’s 21 NP-complete problems with various forms, set cove

