Section name: Experiments
Excerpt:
Having theoretically analyzed the proposed algorithms, in this section we experimentally evaluate the efficiency and efficacy of our algorithms on real and synthetic datasets. Datasets: We use 3 real datasets, one semi-synthesized, and one synthesized dataset to evaluate our methods. Our first set of ex- periments (§ 7.1 and § 7.2) are motivated by Example 1 (Team of Experts Formation), for which we use the Strategeion Resume Skills dataset1. It consists of 1986 rows as candidates with differ- ent skills. The skill of a candidate is shown as 218 binary columns each corresponding to one skill. As an AI Ethics benchmark, the dataset also contains demographic group information, including the (binary) gender. The male-to-female ratio in this dataset is 0.8. Motivated by the "Fair survey data collection" application demon- strated in § 2, we use two additional real datasets: Adult Income andC

Section name: Related Work
Excerpt:
Algorithmic fairness has received a lot of attention in the last few years. However, most of the recent work focuses on the intersection of fairness and Machine Learning [7, 36]. Despite its importance, limited work has been done on algorith- mic fairness in the context of combinatorial problems [ 49]. Satisfy- ing fairness constraints while optimizing sub-modular functions is studied in [ 49]. Further examples include satisfying fairness in Coverage Maximization problems [ 2,4] and Facility Locations [ 29]. In the red-blue set cover problem [ 10], the goal is to select a family of sets to cover all red points, while minimizing the number of blue points covered. In this body of work, the focus is on defining fair- ness on the point set (ground set). In other words, they assume the points belong to different demographic groups. In contrast, in thiswork, we define fairness on the family of

Section name: Conclusion
Excerpt:
Set cover has an extended scope for solving real-world problems with societal impact. In this paper we revisited this problem through the lens of fairness. We adopted the group fairness notion of demo- graphic parity, and proposed a general formulation that extends to various cases such as count-parity and ratio-parity for binary and non-binary demographic groups. We formulated set cover and weighted set cover under fairness constraint and proposed approx- imation algorithms that (a) always guarantee fairness, (b) have (almost) the same approximation ratio and similar time complexity with the the greedy algorithm for set cover (without fairness). Our experiments on real and synthetic data across different settings demonstrated the high performance of our algorithms.

